insert into public.day_recaps (series_id, season_number, week, day, recap) values
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 1, 'Your axle gave on the old forest road, and a silent man came out of the trees, fought you, and was gone, leaving a half-burned charcoal rubbing of a shape you did not know. Nothing was taken from your cargo box. Something had been put in: a list of {N} names in a neat, steady hand, yours among them, and the name of an inn. At the crossroads a thin fragment lay waiting in the stone, cold as a sliver of winter. The Hollow Wake Inn is one day north, and somebody wants you there.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 2, 'At the Hollow Wake Inn the rest of the list was waiting, each of them holding a torn page in the same neat hand. The innkeeper, Garrick, set down one cup more than there were of you, then brought out the satchel of Meira, a scholar who sat in his taproom most nights for two years and has not been back in three weeks. Your pages were cut from her notebook, and two lines in her hand say that the pieces were scattered on purpose, and that the oldest records call the Seekers'' stones shards. Her last readable page tells of a child held near a split rock called the Widow''s Tooth. It lies two hours north, and whatever happened there has not finished.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 3, 'Garrick walked you north to the Widow''s Tooth, grumbling about the road, his boots and the weather, and enjoying every word of it. A watcher in the bracken fought you and ran for the village smoke. Meira''s rubbing fitted a marking low on the rock, line for line: she had been here, and the man on the forest road was carrying her work. Under the overhang lay a child''s carved horse, worn soft with holding. Garrick has not gone back to his inn, and the ruts lead to a village where every door will be shut when you come.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 4, 'Fenmark went quiet as you came down its muddy lane, its far gate still knocking where the lookout had run through. Behind the well a frightened boy told you what he saw: three nights ago quiet men took Tamsin, who is nine and counts when she is frightened, and one of them carried a curved knife with a black handle. The elder would not be seen to help, but under his breath he begged you to find a woman two hours east who has seen that knife before. It was long past noon when Fenmark let you go. The man has half a day on you, and he is heading for the only other person alive who has seen that knife.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 5, 'You reached the old charcoal camp after dark, and the runner from Fenmark was there before you; he fought without a word and fled north. The woman who lives there knew the carved horse at once: she carved it for her sister''s boy, taken by the same black-handled knife two winters ago, at this same time of year. So the child in Meira''s notes was never Tamsin; it was him. By the fire she said one thing twice without hearing herself: the power that gives is also the power that takes. At first light she pointed you north, and if it comes round like a calendar, the time is now.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 6, 'At the ford a child''s small prints came down to the water and stopped where a man''s heels had dug in to lift her, and there the trail ended. On a sun-warmed rock the six cold fragments slid together into one map, ending at the shape from Meira''s rubbing. That night, at a roadside inn, Garrick poured one cup more than there were of you: family habit, he said, one for whoever can''t be with us. The way was read out over supper, and a courier who had come in for a bed paid his bill and left without taking it. Cross at first light, and you will be there by nightfall; whatever is at the place the map marks, the season for it is now.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 1, 7, 'Evening found you at an old mine on the far bank, with torchlight moving inside where no torchlight should be. Garrick knew a way in; he had known it since he was a boy, he said. Over a gallery of rotten timbers, {N} silent men stood between you and a small shape at the back, and none of them spoke while you fought. Then you found Tamsin, wrists tied, humming her counting song, a cut on her palm bound in clean white linen: somebody had taken the time. Behind where they had held her, under a slab of fallen roof, lay a small chamber cut by hands, very old and very still. On its wall was the shape from Meira''s rubbing, and the burned scrap fitted it. But it was not a carving. It was a hollow, made to hold something of that shape, and it was empty. Beneath it, scratched into the stone, were four names, none of them yours, and the first was Tamsin''s. They are children''s names. This was never about you, and why your names were on that list, the wall does not say. Garrick counted the names twice in silence and said, “Three more.” Then, from the mine mouth, came hooves: one horse at full gallop, going away.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 1, 'Tamsin went home to Fenmark on a carter''s wagon, but first she told you what she heard while she was held: “The mill boy next.” The second name on the mine wall is Corin, and Meira''s notes place his family at a working mill. Garrick, who had agreed to come only as far as the Widow''s Tooth, shouldered a pack and came too. At the last farmhouse an old man told you a cart with no lamps went up to the mill three nights ago and came back before dawn, turning upriver: the same people who took Tamsin. The mill is an hour on; at first light, what did the family see?'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 2, 'The mill wheel was still turning, and the old stone under it carries the shape from the burned rubbing. The miller''s wife told you a big, quiet man took her boy Corin four nights ago and never hid his face; he asked for Corin by name, and by the night he was born. She pressed a horn button into your hand, with a twist of dark thread still through it. They are not taking any child who comes to hand; they are taking particular ones. The ruts go upriver, four nights old, and there is weather coming.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 3, 'After a night of rain the ruts led upriver until the bank turned to stone, and a hidden path climbed into the hillside past a great dog on a new chain. Beyond it lay a stranger a week dead, his pick still jammed in an old stone lock built into the hill. The lock would not open for him. It opened at once for you, and there was a cold fragment inside. Somebody on the slope above was watching, and the path goes on past the dead man, and up.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 4, 'The path ended at a timbered shaft, with a ladder going down into lamplight and voices below saying the same words together. Corin is down there, alive, in a chamber cut into the hill, held by hooded figures who are making something ready. Then a shout went up, you were driven back, and the ladder was hauled away: they know you are here. Is there another way into this hill before they finish? You have tonight to find it.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 5, 'There is no second door on the hillside. Meira''s margin says why the old fragments never name the man they speak of: every record of him was scraped clean, because someone wanted him forgotten. A hooded scout fought you without a word and tore free, leaving in your fist a strip of his coat with a small black circle sewn to it, and at its cuff a snapped thread, the same twist as the one through the horn button from the mill. Before the dark took him he opened a lamp twice toward the shaft. Now there is a clock: last week six cold slivers showed you a way nobody was watching, and you have five.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 6, 'On your second night at the lip of the shaft the chanting started again, and the light below said they were nearly ready. The sixth fragment lay among the roots, not hidden at all, and the six made a map again: an older room, deeper in the same hill, with a shaft of its own. The way was read aloud twice across the dark, so that everyone on watch had it. Two hundred paces along, under a fall of old stone, you found that shaft''s mouth unguarded; within the hour, far down in the rock, the picks began. They are digging toward the old room: be in it first.'),
('dark-moon', 1, 2, 7, 'Down the old shaft and a stair worn soft with age, you came to the oldest room under the mill hill, where robed figures chanted round a stone table with Corin on it. You fought them, and Corin is alive, but his palm was already cut and bound in white linen: whatever they needed from him, they had taken. Behind the table was another socket, cut with the same care and empty, the dust at its rim barely disturbed, and the burned rubbing fitted it. Beside the table lay their scripture, a small black book with a black circle on its first page, the same circle as the scrap from the scout''s coat. It tells of a war that ate the world, and of one king on the last wall who would not kneel, and who gave his own blood on the night the moon went dark. The world he saved took back his name. His faithful remember him, and give the marked blood in his name. In a niche by a lamp lay a ledger of children''s names, places and dates, and its newest page held the four names from the mine wall. The marked are those born on a night the moon went dark, as Corin was. Meira wrote your birth nights beside your names, and they are dark-moon nights too: you are marked, every one of you. Garrick read the ledger, tucked it inside his coat, and said, “This hand''s been keeping books like this for years. This isn''t new, and it isn''t finished. Two places. Two children.” Then Corin told you about a girl held down there with him, taken away not long before you came. She said they told her she was going somewhere warm.')
